{"question": "A 67-year-old man with peripheral neuropathy comes to the physician for a follow-up examination after the results of serum protein electrophoresis showed monoclonal gammopathy. A complete blood count, serum creatinine, and serum electrolyte concentrations are within the reference ranges. A bone marrow biopsy shows 6% monoclonal plasma cells. Further analysis shows that class I major histocompatibility molecules are downregulated in these monoclonal plasma cells. The proliferation of these monoclonal plasma cells is normally prevented by a class of immune cells that lyse abnormal cells without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation. Which of the following best describes this class of immune cells?", "options": {"A": "CD8+ T lymphocytes", "B": "Bone marrow-derived macrophages", "C": "CD4+ T lymphocytes", "D": "Natural killer cells", "E": "Band neutrophils"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Hematology", "Immunology", "Oncology", "Neurology", "Laboratory Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Immunology", "Hematology"], "question_analyses": {"Hematology": "The medical scenario described involves a 67-year-old male patient with peripheral neuropathy who has been found to have monoclonal gammopathy on serum protein electrophoresis. Monoclonal gammopathy refers to the presence of a single type of immunoglobulin (paraprotein) in the blood, which is often indicative of a plasma cell disorder. The patient's complete blood count, serum creatinine, and serum electrolyte concentrations are within normal ranges, suggesting that these parameters are not currently contributing to his condition. However, the bone marrow biopsy reveals a 6% monoclonal plasma cell population, which is a significant finding. The downregulation of class I major histocompatibility (MHC) molecules in the monoclonal plasma cells is particularly noteworthy. Class I MHC molecules are crucial for the recognition of abnormal cells by certain immune cells, s